[pfcwd] batch ingress IN_PORTS updates across a storm event batch

SAI has no incremental in-ports update: every write replaces an ACL rule's
whole port list. The drop action added one port per storm, so N ports storming
the same lossless queue cost N(N+1) port-OID writes across the two shared
ingress rules, all of it inside the SAI mutex. Measured on a 26-port Sea Eagle
the count matched N(N+1) exactly at every point: 6, 30, 110, 240, 420 and 650
writes for 2, 5, 10, 15, 20 and 25 ports.

Drain the watchdog notification queue with pops() so a congestion event that
moves many queues is handled as one batch, and have the ACL handler record its
intended in-ports changes instead of programming each one. A flush at the end
of the batch computes each rule's final port set and writes it once, turning
the cost into O(N). Last write wins per port, so a queue that storms and
restores inside one batch nets out correctly, and a rule left with no ports is
removed rather than written empty - an empty IN_PORTS list means "field
disabled", which would make a drop rule match every port.

AclRule::setInPorts() sets the list straight from an OID vector, so the alias
string that the old path built and reparsed on every single-port change is gone
too.

The flush also runs wherever handlers are created or destroyed in bulk:
big-red-switch enable/disable, the warm-restart replay, and port
unregistration. The rule lookup that the destructor used to do (and throw on)
moves into the flush, which is where the choice between dropping one port and
removing the rule can be made for the whole batch.

Upstream-status: not submitted
